            <ArticleTitle>Economic evaluation of an occupational health care guideline for prevention of weight gain among employees.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1100-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/JOM.0b013e31829b279a</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the cost-effectiveness and the financial return of a guideline for occupational physicians (OPs), aimed at the prevention of weight gain among employees.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The guideline consisted of a company-environment scan and up to five individual sessions with an OP. Sixteen OPs randomized to the guideline (n = 7) or usual care group (n = 9) recruited 275 and 249 employees. Costs were collected by using 3-monthly retrospective questionnaires. Quality-adjusted life-years gained were determined with 6-monthly administered EuroQol-5D. Waist circumference and body weight were measured at baseline and 18 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Unfavorable differences were found on waist circumference (+1.6 cm; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.27 to 2.90) and weight (+1.1 kg; 95% CI, 0.01 to 2.15). Probabilities of cost-effectiveness were consistently less than 55%. Net employer loss was &amp;OV0556;-158 (95% CI, -2865 to 2672).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The occupational health care guideline was not cost-effective and no financial return was shown.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
